(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Circuit Analysis I"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Circuit Analysis I"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Circuit Analysis I: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nDraw every circuit, label nodes and reference directions, and state which method you use. Check each answer with KCL/KVL or a power balance. All numerical results below were verified by computer.\n\n# Assignment 1: DC Circuit Analysis (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Basics (8 marks)** A 36 V source feeds 4 Ω in series with (6 Ω ∥ 12 Ω) in series with 2 Ω. Find the total resistance, the source current, the voltage across the parallel pair, the current and power in each of the 6 Ω and 12 Ω resistors, and the total power. Verify the power balance.\n\n**Q2. Node-voltage method (10 marks)** A 5 A source drives node 1 and a 2 A source drives node 2 (currents flow into the nodes). A 2 Ω resistor connects node 1 to ground, a 4 Ω resistor connects nodes 1 and 2, and an 8 Ω resistor connects node 2 to ground. Find v1 and v2, the current in the 4 Ω resistor and the power delivered by each source.\n\n**Q3. Mesh-current method (10 marks)** Mesh 1 contains a 10 V source, 2 Ω and the shared 4 Ω resistor; mesh 2 contains 6 Ω, the shared 4 Ω and a 4 V source whose polarity opposes the clockwise current. Find the mesh currents, the current in the shared resistor and the power of each source.\n\n**Q4. Thevenin, Norton and maximum power (12 marks)** A 12 V source in series with 6 Ω feeds node a; 3 Ω connects a to ground; a 2 A source injects current into node a. The load is connected between a and ground. (a) Find V_th, R_th and I_N [5]. (b) Find the load voltage and power for R_L = 6 Ω [3]. (c) Find R_L for maximum power and the maximum power [2]. (d) Find the efficiency at maximum power (power in R_L as a fraction of the power generated inside the equivalent) [2].\n\n**Q5. Dependent source (8 marks)** A 1 A source feeds node 1; 2 Ω connects node 1 to ground; 4 Ω connects nodes 1 and 2; 2 Ω connects node 2 to ground; a dependent current source of 0.5 v1 amperes flows from node 2 to ground. Find v1 and v2.\n\n**Q6. Operational amplifiers (12 marks)** The supply rails are ±12 V. Find v_o for: (a) an inverting amplifier with R_f = 100 kΩ, R_in = 10 kΩ and v_in = 0.5 V, and again for v_in = 1.5 V [3]; (b) a non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 100 kΩ, R_g = 10 kΩ, v_in = 0.5 V [2]; (c) a summing amplifier with R_f = 100 kΩ, R1 = 50 kΩ (v1 = 1 V), R2 = 25 kΩ (v2 = 2 V) [3]; (d) a difference amplifier with R1 = 10 kΩ and R2 = 20 kΩ (gain R2/R1 on v2 - v1), v1 = 1.5 V and v2 = 2.0 V [2]; (e) an integrator with R = 10 kΩ and C = 0.1 μF with a 2 V step applied at t = 0; when does the output saturate? [2]\n\n**Q7. Bridges and Y-Δ (10 marks)** (a) A Wheatstone bridge is driven by 10 V; the left divider is 100 Ω/100 Ω and the right divider is 100 Ω (top) and 105 Ω (bottom). Find the bridge output voltage between the divider midpoints [3]. (b) Convert a Δ of three equal 30 Ω resistors into a Y [2]. (c) A bridge has 10 Ω from the source top to node a, 20 Ω from the top to node b, 30 Ω from a to ground, 40 Ω from b to ground and a 5 Ω bridge resistor between a and b. Find the equivalent resistance seen by the source [5].\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\nStep 1 (parallel pair): 6 Ω ∥ 12 Ω = (6 × 12)/(6 + 12) = 72/18 = 4 Ω.\nStep 2 (total resistance): R_eq = 4 + 4 + 2 = **10 Ω** (series).\nStep 3 (source current): I = 36/10 = **3.6 A**; this current flows through the 4 Ω, the parallel pair and the 2 Ω.\nStep 4 (parallel voltage): V = I × 4 Ω = 3.6 × 4 = **14.4 V**.\nStep 5 (branch currents): I₆ = 14.4/6 = **2.4 A**, I₁₂ = 14.4/12 = **1.2 A**. Check: 2.4 + 1.2 = 3.6 ✓.\nStep 6 (powers, P = I²R): P₄ = 3.6² × 4 = 51.84 W; P₆ = 2.4² × 6 = 34.56 W; P₁₂ = 1.2² × 12 = 17.28 W; P₂ = 3.6² × 2 = 25.92 W.\nStep 7 (balance): total absorbed = 51.84 + 34.56 + 17.28 + 25.92 = 129.6 W. Source power = 36 × 3.6 = 129.6 W ✓.\n(2 marks R_eq, 2 currents, 2 voltages, 2 power balance.)\n\n**Q2.** Unknowns v₁, v₂ (node voltages relative to ground).\nStep 1 (KCL at node 1, currents leaving = 5 A entering): v₁/2 + (v₁ - v₂)/4 = 5, i.e. 0.5v₁ + 0.25v₁ - 0.25v₂ = 5, so 0.75v₁ - 0.25v₂ = 5.\nStep 2 (KCL at node 2): (v₂ - v₁)/4 + v₂/8 = 2, i.e. -0.25v₁ + 0.25v₂ + 0.125v₂ = 2, so -0.25v₁ + 0.375v₂ = 2.\nStep 3 (clear fractions, multiply both by 8): 6v₁ - 2v₂ = 40 and -2v₁ + 3v₂ = 16.\nStep 4 (substitute): from the first, v₂ = 3v₁ - 20. Into the second: -2v₁ + 3(3v₁ - 20) = 16, so 7v₁ - 60 = 16 and 7v₁ = 76.\nStep 5: **v₁ = 76/7 = 10.86 V**; v₂ = 3(76/7) - 20 = 228/7 - 140/7 = 88/7 = **12.57 V**.\nStep 6 (4 Ω current from node 1 to 2): (10.857 - 12.571)/4 = **-0.429 A** (so 0.429 A actually flows from node 2 to node 1).\nStep 7 (source powers, P = v × I): 5 A source: 5 × 10.857 = **54.3 W**; 2 A source: 2 × 12.571 = **25.1 W**; total 79.4 W.\nStep 8 (check absorbed): 10.857²/2 = 58.9 W; 12.571²/8 = 19.8 W; 0.429² × 4 = 0.73 W; total 79.4 W ✓.\n\n**Q3.** Clockwise mesh currents i₁ (mesh 1) and i₂ (mesh 2); the shared 4 Ω carries i₁ - i₂ (as seen from mesh 1).\nStep 1 (KVL mesh 1): 2i₁ + 4(i₁ - i₂) = 10, so 6i₁ - 4i₂ = 10.\nStep 2 (KVL mesh 2, the 4 V source opposes i₂ so it is a +4 V drop): 6i₂ + 4(i₂ - i₁) + 4 = 0, so -4i₁ + 10i₂ = -4.\nStep 3 (solve by elimination): multiply the first by 5: 30i₁ - 20i₂ = 50; multiply the second by 2: -8i₁ + 20i₂ = -8. Add: 22i₁ = 42.\nStep 4: **i₁ = 42/22 = 21/11 = 1.909 A**. From the first equation: 4i₂ = 6(1.909) - 10 = 1.455, so **i₂ = 0.364 A (= 4/11)**.\nStep 5 (shared resistor): i₁ - i₂ = 1.909 - 0.364 = **1.545 A**.\nStep 6 (source powers): the 10 V source delivers 10 × 1.909 = **19.09 W**. The 4 V source: the current i₂ = 0.364 A flows into its positive terminal in this loop (it opposes i₂), so it absorbs 4 × 0.364 = **1.455 W** (it is being charged).\nStep 7 (check): resistor power = 1.909² × 2 + 0.364² × 6 + 1.545² × 4 = 7.29 + 0.79 + 9.55 = 17.63 W, and 19.09 - 1.455 = 17.63 W ✓.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1 (V_th, open circuit at node a): KCL: (v_a - 12)/6 + v_a/3 = 2.\nStep 2: multiply by 6: (v_a - 12) + 2v_a = 12, so 3v_a = 24 and **V_th = 8 V**.\nStep 3 (R_th, sources off: voltage source shorted, current source opened): R_th = 6 ∥ 3 = 18/9 = **2 Ω**.\nStep 4: I_N = V_th/R_th = 8/2 = **4 A**. (Check by shorting a to ground: 12/6 = 2 A from the voltage branch plus the 2 A source = 4 A ✓.)\n(b) Step 1: R_L = 6 Ω: V_L = V_th R_L/(R_th + R_L) = 8 × 6/(2 + 6) = **6 V**.\nStep 2: P_L = V_L²/R_L = 36/6 = **6 W**.\n(c) Step 1: maximum power transfer when R_L = R_th = **2 Ω**.\nStep 2: V_L = 8/2 = 4 V and P_max = V_th²/(4R_th) = 64/8 = **8 W**.\n(d) Step 1: at R_L = R_th the current is I = 8/(2 + 2) = 2 A. The Thevenin source supplies V_th × I = 8 × 2 = 16 W.\nStep 2: efficiency = 8/16 = **50 percent**. (This describes the equivalent; it does not give the real network's internal dissipation.)\n\n**Q5.** Node 1 current sources: 1 A in. The dependent source 0.5v₁ flows from node 2 to ground.\nStep 1 (KCL node 1): v₁/2 + (v₁ - v₂)/4 = 1, i.e. 0.75v₁ - 0.25v₂ = 1.\nStep 2 (KCL node 2, leaving currents): (v₂ - v₁)/4 + v₂/2 + 0.5v₁ = 0, i.e. -0.25v₁ + 0.5v₁ + 0.25v₂ + 0.5v₂ = 0, so 0.25v₁ + 0.75v₂ = 0.\nStep 3: from step 2, v₁ = -3v₂.\nStep 4: substitute in step 1: 0.75(-3v₂) - 0.25v₂ = 1, so -2.25v₂ - 0.25v₂ = -2.5v₂ = 1 and **v₂ = -0.4 V**.\nStep 5: **v₁ = -3(-0.4) = 1.2 V**.\nStep 6 (check node 1): 1.2/2 + (1.2 + 0.4)/4 = 0.6 + 0.4 = 1 ✓.\n\n**Q6.** Ideal op-amp rules: v₊ = v₋ and no input current; output limited to ±12 V.\n(a) Inverting: v_o = -(R_f/R_in) v_in = -(100/10) × 0.5 = **-5 V**. For v_in = 1.5 V the ideal result is -10 × 1.5 = -15 V, which exceeds the rail, so the output **saturates at -12 V**.\n(b) Non-inverting: gain = 1 + R_f/R_g = 1 + 100/10 = 11; v_o = 11 × 0.5 = **5.5 V**.\n(c) Summing: v_o = -R_f(v₁/R₁ + v₂/R₂) = -100 k(1/50 k + 2/25 k) = -100(0.02 + 0.08) = **-10 V** (inside the rails).\n(d) Difference amplifier: v_o = (R₂/R₁)(v₂ - v₁) = 2(2.0 - 1.5) = **1.0 V**.\n(e) Integrator: RC = 10⁴ × 10⁻⁷ = 1 ms. v_o = -(1/RC) ∫ v_in dt = -(1/0.001)(2t) = -2000t volts. Set -2000t = -12: **t = 6 ms**, after which the output stays saturated at -12 V.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) Step 1: left midpoint = 10 × 100/(100 + 100) = 5.000 V.\nStep 2: right midpoint (across the lower 105 Ω) = 10 × 105/(100 + 105) = 10 × 0.51220 = 5.122 V.\nStep 3: bridge output = 5.000 - 5.122 = **-0.122 V**. A 5 percent change in one arm gives only about 1.2 percent of the supply; bridges are used with an amplifier for strain gauges and sensors.\n(b) Δ to Y with equal resistors: R_Y = R_Δ R_Δ/(R_Δ + R_Δ + R_Δ) = 30 × 30/90 = **10 Ω** for each leg (R_Y = R_Δ/3).\n(c) Apply 1 V to the source terminal and find the current; node voltages v_a and v_b.\nStep 1 (KCL at a): (v_a - 1)/10 + v_a/30 + (v_a - v_b)/5 = 0. Multiply by 30: 3(v_a - 1) + v_a + 6(v_a - v_b) = 0, so 10v_a - 6v_b = 3.\nStep 2 (KCL at b): (v_b - 1)/20 + v_b/40 + (v_b - v_a)/5 = 0. Multiply by 40: 2(v_b - 1) + v_b + 8(v_b - v_a) = 0, so -8v_a + 11v_b = 2.\nStep 3 (solve): from step 1, v_b = (10v_a - 3)/6. Substitute: -8v_a + 11(10v_a - 3)/6 = 2 gives -48v_a + 110v_a - 33 = 12, so 62v_a = 45 and v_a = 0.7258 V; v_b = (7.258 - 3)/6 = 0.7097 V.\nStep 4 (source current): I = (1 - v_a)/10 + (1 - v_b)/20 = 0.02742 + 0.01452 = 0.04194 A.\nStep 5: **R_eq = 1/0.04194 = 23.85 Ω (= 310/13)**.\nCheck: the bridge resistor carries (0.7258 - 0.7097)/5 = 3.2 mA from a to b.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Capacitors, Inductors and Transients (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Storage elements (8 marks)** (a) A 2 μF capacitor has v(t) = 5t^2 V. Find i(t) and the stored energy at t = 2 s [3]. (b) Find the energy in a 0.5 H inductor carrying 4 A [1]. (c) Three capacitors of 6 μF, 3 μF and 2 μF are in series; find C_eq, and the voltage on each if the combination is charged to 12 V [4].\n\n**Q2. RC step response (8 marks)** A 10 V source charges a 500 μF capacitor through 2 kΩ; v_C(0) = 2 V. Find v_C(t), the time to reach 9 V, the current at t = 0+ and the energy stored at 9 V.\n\n**Q3. First-order circuit with Thevenin reduction (10 marks)** A 12 V source feeds a 6 kΩ resistor; 3 kΩ connects the node to ground, with a 100 μF capacitor across the 3 kΩ resistor. The capacitor is uncharged at t = 0 when the switch closes. Find V_th, R_th, τ, v_C(t) and the time to reach 90 percent of the final value.\n\n**Q4. RL switching (10 marks)** A 0.4 H inductor in series with 8 Ω carries steady current from a 24 V source. At t = 0 the source is removed and an extra 8 Ω resistor is inserted in series in the closed loop. Find i(0+), τ, i(t), the inductor voltage at t = 0+ and the total energy dissipated.\n\n**Q5. Pulse input (6 marks)** A 10 V pulse of duration 1 s is applied to an RC circuit with τ = 0.5 s (initially uncharged). Find v_C(1 s) and v_C(2 s).\n\n**Q6. Second-order circuits (16 marks)** A series RLC circuit is driven by a 10 V step with zero initial energy; v_C is the output. (a) R = 4 Ω, L = 1 H, C = 1/5 F: classify and find v_C(t) [5]. (b) R = 6 Ω, L = 1 H, C = 1/8 F: classify and find v_C(t) [4]. (c) R = 4 Ω, L = 1 H, C = 1/4 F: classify and find v_C(t) [4]. (d) For L = 0.1 H and C = 10 μF find the value of R giving critical damping [3].\n\n**Q7. Energy (6 marks)** A camera flash capacitor of 120 μF is charged to 300 V and discharged in 2 ms. Find the stored energy and the average power delivered.\n\n**Q8. Measurement (6 marks)** The output of an RC circuit driven by a step to 5 V is measured on an oscilloscope: it reads 3.16 V at t = 3 ms. Find τ, and C if R = 3 kΩ; find the time to reach 99 percent of the final value.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: v(t) = 5t², so dv/dt = 10t V/s.\nStep 2: i = C dv/dt = (2 × 10⁻⁶)(10t) = 20 × 10⁻⁶ t = **20t μA**.\nStep 3: at t = 2 s: v = 5(4) = 20 V.\nStep 4: w = (1/2)Cv² = (1/2)(2 × 10⁻⁶)(20)² = (1 × 10⁻⁶)(400) = **0.4 mJ**.\n(b) w = (1/2)Li² = (1/2)(0.5)(4)² = (0.25)(16) = **4 J**.\n(c) Step 1: series capacitors: 1/C_eq = 1/6 + 1/3 + 1/2 = 1/6 + 2/6 + 3/6 = 6/6 = 1 μF⁻¹, so **C_eq = 1 μF**.\nStep 2: series capacitors share the same charge: Q = C_eq V = 1 μF × 12 V = 12 μC.\nStep 3: voltages V = Q/C: 12/6 = **2 V**, 12/3 = **4 V**, 12/2 = **6 V**. Check: 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 V ✓. The smallest capacitor takes the largest voltage.\n\n**Q2.** RC charging with initial value 2 V and final value 10 V.\nStep 1: τ = RC = (2000 Ω)(500 × 10⁻⁶ F) = **1 s**.\nStep 2: v_C(t) = v(∞) + [v(0) - v(∞)]e^(-t/τ) = 10 + (2 - 10)e^(-t) = **10 - 8e^(-t) V**.\nStep 3: set v_C = 9: 10 - 8e^(-t) = 9, so e^(-t) = 1/8 and t = ln 8 = **2.08 s**.\nStep 4: current at 0⁺: i = (V_s - v_C(0))/R = (10 - 2)/2000 = **4 mA**.\nStep 5: energy at 9 V: w = (1/2)Cv² = (1/2)(500 × 10⁻⁶)(81) = **20.25 mJ**.\n\n**Q3.** The capacitor sees the Thevenin equivalent of the source and the divider (6 kΩ in series with the source, 3 kΩ to ground).\nStep 1: V_th = 12 × 3/(6 + 3) = **4 V** (voltage divider, capacitor removed).\nStep 2: R_th = 6 kΩ ∥ 3 kΩ = (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = **2 kΩ** (source shorted).\nStep 3: τ = R_th C = (2000)(100 × 10⁻⁶) = **0.2 s**.\nStep 4: uncharged capacitor, so v_C(t) = V_th(1 - e^(-t/τ)) = **4(1 - e^(-5t)) V**.\nStep 5: 90 percent of the final value: 1 - e^(-5t) = 0.9, so e^(-5t) = 0.1 and t = ln 10/5 = 2.303/5 = **0.461 s**.\n\n**Q4.**\nStep 1 (before t = 0, steady state, inductor is a short): i = 24/8 = 3 A.\nStep 2 (continuity of inductor current): **i(0⁺) = 3 A**.\nStep 3 (after the change the loop has L = 0.4 H and R = 8 + 8 = 16 Ω): **τ = L/R = 0.4/16 = 0.025 s = 25 ms**.\nStep 4: i(t) = i(0⁺)e^(-t/τ) = **3e^(-40t) A**.\nStep 5 (inductor voltage at 0⁺): v_L = -i R_total = -3 × 16 = **-48 V** (a large reversed voltage; switches need protection).\nStep 6 (energy): all the initial stored energy is dissipated: w = (1/2)Li² = (1/2)(0.4)(9) = **1.8 J**, shared in proportion to resistance (0.9 J in each 8 Ω resistor).\n\n**Q5.** RC circuit, τ = 0.5 s, 10 V pulse for 0 ≤ t ≤ 1 s, starting at 0 V.\nStep 1 (during the pulse): v_C(t) = 10(1 - e^(-t/0.5)) = 10(1 - e^(-2t)).\nStep 2: at t = 1 s: e^(-2) = 0.1353, so v_C(1) = 10(1 - 0.1353) = **8.647 V**.\nStep 3 (after the pulse the source is 0 V): v_C(t) = 8.647 e^(-2(t - 1)).\nStep 4: at t = 2 s: 8.647 × e^(-2) = 8.647 × 0.1353 = **1.170 V**.\n\n**Q6.** Series RLC with step input: s² + (R/L)s + 1/(LC) = 0; final value v_C(∞) = 10 V; v_C(0) = 0 and v_C'(0) = i(0)/C = 0.\n(a) Step 1: R = 4, L = 1, C = 1/5: s² + 4s + 5 = 0.\nStep 2: Discriminant 16 - 20 = -4 < 0: s = (-4 ± j2)/2 = -2 ± j1.\nStep 3: **Underdamped** (α = 2, ω_d = 1).\nStep 1: v_C = 10 + e^(-2t)(A cos t + B sin t).\nStep 2: v_C(0) = 10 + A = 0, so A = -10.\nStep 3: v_C'(0) = -2A + B = 0, so B = 2A = -20.\n**v_C = 10 - e^(-2t)(10 cos t + 20 sin t) V**.\n(b) R = 6, L = 1, C = 1/8: s² + 6s + 8 = (s + 2)(s + 4) = 0, s = -2, -4: **overdamped**.\nStep 1: v_C = 10 + Ae^(-2t) + Be^(-4t).\nStep 2: v_C(0) = 10 + A + B = 0, so A + B = -10.\nStep 3: v_C'(0) = -2A - 4B = 0, so A = -2B.\nStep 4: -2B + B = -10, so B = 10 and A = -20.\n**v_C = 10 - 20e^(-2t) + 10e^(-4t) V**. Check: v_C(0) = 10 - 20 + 10 = 0 ✓; v_C'(0) = 40 - 40 = 0 ✓.\n(c) R = 4, L = 1, C = 1/4: s² + 4s + 4 = (s + 2)² = 0: repeated root -2, **critically damped**.\nStep 1: v_C = 10 + (A + Bt)e^(-2t).\nStep 2: v_C(0) = 10 + A = 0, so A = -10.\nStep 3: v_C'(0) = -2A + B = 0, so B = 2A = -20.\n**v_C = 10 - (10 + 20t)e^(-2t) V**.\n(d) Critical damping needs the discriminant zero: (R/L)² = 4/(LC), i.e. R² = 4L/C.\nStep 1: R = 2√(L/C) = 2√(0.1/10⁻⁵) = 2√(10⁴) = 2 × 100.\n**R = 200 Ω**.\n\n**Q7.** Step 1: w = (1/2)CV² = (1/2)(120 × 10⁻⁶)(300)² = (60 × 10⁻⁶)(90,000) = **5.4 J**.\nStep 2: average power = w/t = 5.4/0.002 = **2,700 W = 2.7 kW**. (A small capacitor can deliver very large power briefly because the energy is released quickly.)\n\n**Q8.** Step 1: the reading as a fraction of the final value: 3.16/5 = 0.632 = 1 - e^(-1), so the measurement was made at one time constant: **τ = 3 ms**.\nStep 2: C = τ/R = (3 × 10⁻³)/3000 = **1 μF**.\nStep 3: 99 percent of the final value: 1 - e^(-t/τ) = 0.99, so e^(-t/τ) = 0.01 and t = τ ln 100 = 4.605τ = 4.605 × 3 ms = **13.8 ms**.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Sinusoidal Steady State, AC Power and Frequency Response (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Phasors (8 marks)** (a) Express 10 cos(1000t + 30°) V and 5 cos(1000t - 60°) V as phasors and find their sum as a time function [3]. (b) Add i1 = 4 cos(ωt + 20°) A and i2 = 3 sin(ωt) A [3]. (c) The rms value of a 325 V peak sine wave [2].\n\n**Q2. Impedance (10 marks)** At ω = 1000 rad/s: (a) Find the impedance of a 30 Ω resistor in series with 40 mH, and the current for 100∠0° V [4]. (b) Find the impedance of a 20 mH inductor in parallel with a 100 μF capacitor, in series with 10 Ω [4]. (c) State the phase of the current relative to the voltage in each case [2].\n\n**Q3. AC circuit analysis (10 marks)** A 100∠0° V (rms) source feeds an impedance Z1 = 10 + j10 Ω in series with the parallel combination of a -j20 Ω capacitor and a 20 Ω resistor. Find the total impedance, the source current, the voltage across the parallel part, the current in each parallel branch, and the complex power delivered by the source.\n\n**Q4. AC power (12 marks)** (a) A load connected to 230 V rms, 50 Hz absorbs 5 kW at power factor 0.8 lagging. Find S, Q, the line current [4]. (b) Find the capacitor in parallel that raises the power factor to 0.95 lagging, and the new line current [5]. (c) Explain why utilities penalise low power factor [3].\n\n**Q5. Resonance (10 marks)** A series RLC circuit has R = 10 Ω, L = 10 mH, C = 1 μF. Find ω0 and f0, the quality factor, the bandwidth, the current at resonance for a 1 V source and the voltages across L and C at resonance.\n\n**Q6. Frequency response (10 marks)** An RC low-pass filter has R = 1 kΩ and C = 0.1 μF. (a) Find the cut-off frequency [2]. (b) Find the gain in dB and the phase at f_c and at 10 f_c [4]. (c) Sketch the Bode magnitude and state the roll-off [2]. (d) How would you build a high-pass filter with the same cut-off? [2]\n\n**Q7. Maximum power transfer (6 marks)** A source has V_th = 20 V rms and Z_th = 3 + j4 Ω. Find the load impedance that receives maximum power and the power delivered. What if the load must be purely resistive?\n\n**Q8. Three-phase (8 marks)** A balanced Y-connected load with Z = 20∠30° Ω per phase is supplied with 400 V line-to-line, 50 Hz. Find the phase voltage, line current, the total real, reactive and apparent power and the power factor.\n\n**Q9. Transformer (6 marks)** An ideal transformer has 2300 V on the primary and 230 V on the secondary feeding a 5 Ω load. Find the secondary and primary currents, the load power and the load impedance reflected to the primary.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: phasors (peak): V₁ = 10∠30°, V₂ = 5∠-60°.\nStep 2: rectangular form: V₁ = 10 cos 30° + j10 sin 30° = 8.660 + j5.000; V₂ = 5 cos(-60°) + j5 sin(-60°) = 2.500 - j4.330.\nStep 3: add: 8.660 + 2.500 = 11.160 and 5.000 - 4.330 = 0.670, so V = 11.160 + j0.670.\nStep 4: polar: |V| = √(11.160² + 0.670²) = 11.18 and angle = arctan(0.670/11.160) = 3.44°.\n**v(t) = 11.18 cos(1000t + 3.4°) V**.\n(b) Step 1: convert sine to cosine: 3 sin ωt = 3 cos(ωt - 90°), so I₂ = 3∠-90° = -j3.\nStep 2: I₁ = 4∠20° = 4 cos 20° + j4 sin 20° = 3.759 + j1.368.\nStep 3: sum = 3.759 + (1.368 - 3)j = 3.759 - j1.632.\nStep 4: magnitude √(3.759² + 1.632²) = 4.10, angle = arctan(-1.632/3.759) = -23.5°. **i = 4.10 cos(ωt - 23.5°) A**.\n(c) V_rms = V_peak/√2 = 325/1.4142 = **230 V**.\n\n**Q2.** ω = 1000 rad/s.\n(a) Step 1: X_L = ωL = 1000 × 0.040 = 40 Ω, so Z = 30 + j40 Ω.\nStep 2: polar: |Z| = √(30² + 40²) = 50 Ω, angle = arctan(40/30) = 53.13°. **Z = 50∠53.13° Ω**.\nStep 3: I = V/Z = 100∠0°/50∠53.13° = **2∠-53.13° A** (the current lags: inductive).\n(b) Step 1: Z_L = jωL = j(1000)(0.020) = j20 Ω; Z_C = 1/(jωC) = 1/(j × 1000 × 100 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/(j0.1) = -j10 Ω.\nStep 2: parallel: Z_p = (j20)(-j10)/(j20 - j10) = (200)/(j10) = -j20 Ω (since (j)(-j) = 1).\nStep 3: add the 10 Ω in series: **Z = 10 - j20 = 22.36∠-63.4° Ω** (capacitive, because the capacitor dominates the parallel pair).\n(c) (a): current **lags** the voltage by 53.1°. (b): current **leads** the voltage by 63.4° (negative impedance angle).\n\n**Q3.**\nStep 1 (parallel part): Z_p = (20)(-j20)/(20 - j20) = -j400/(20 - j20). Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate (20 + j20): -j400(20 + j20)/(400 + 400) = (-j8000 + 8000)/800 = 10 - j10 Ω.\nStep 2 (total): Z_total = (10 + j10) + (10 - j10) = **20 Ω** (purely resistive: the reactances cancel).\nStep 3 (current): I = 100∠0°/20 = **5∠0° A**.\nStep 4 (parallel voltage): V_p = I Z_p = 5(10 - j10) = 50 - j50 = **70.7∠-45° V**.\nStep 5 (branch currents): I_R = V_p/20 = 2.5 - j2.5 = **3.54∠-45° A**. I_C = V_p/(-j20) = (50 - j50)/(-j20) = (50 - j50)(j)/20 = (j50 + 50)/20 = 2.5 + j2.5 = **3.54∠+45° A**.\nStep 6 (check KCL): I_R + I_C = (2.5 - j2.5) + (2.5 + j2.5) = 5 A ✓.\nStep 7 (complex power): S = V I* = (100∠0°)(5∠0°) = **500 + j0 VA** (P = 500 W, Q = 0).\nCheck: I²R_total = 5² × 20 = 500 W ✓.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Step 1: S = P/pf = 5000/0.8 = **6.25 kVA**.\nStep 2: Q = √(S² - P²) = √(6250² - 5000²) = √(39.06 × 10⁶ - 25 × 10⁶) = √(14.06 × 10⁶) = **3750 var**.\nStep 3: I = S/V = 6250/230 = **27.2 A**.\n(b) Step 1: target pf 0.95 gives tan(arccos 0.95) = tan(18.19°) = 0.3287, so Q_new = P tan φ = 5000 × 0.3287 = 1643 var.\nStep 2: the capacitor must supply Q_c = 3750 - 1643 = **2107 var**.\nStep 3: Q_c = ωCV² with ω = 2π(50) = 314.16 rad/s: C = 2107/(314.16 × 230²) = 2107/(314.16 × 52,900) = 2107/16.62 × 10⁶ = **127 μF**.\nStep 4: new current I = P/(V pf) = 5000/(230 × 0.95) = **22.9 A** (down from 27.2 A, a 16 percent reduction; the I²R line loss falls by 1 - (22.9/27.2)² = 29 percent).\n(c) The real power delivered is the same, but a low power factor makes the line carry extra reactive current. This raises conductor and transformer losses (I²R) and voltage drop and needs larger cables and equipment, without producing useful energy, so utilities charge for kVA or reactive energy or penalise low power factor.\n\n**Q5.**\nStep 1: ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(0.01 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(10⁻⁸) = **10⁴ rad/s**; f₀ = ω₀/(2π) = **1592 Hz**.\nStep 2: Q = ω₀L/R = (10⁴)(0.01)/10 = **10**.\nStep 3: bandwidth BW = ω₀/Q = 10⁴/10 = **1000 rad/s (159 Hz)**.\nStep 4: at resonance Z = R, so I = V/R = 1/10 = **0.1 A**.\nStep 5: V_L = I ω₀L = 0.1 × 100 = **10 V**; V_C = I/(ω₀C) = 0.1 × (1/(10⁴ × 10⁻⁶)) = 0.1 × 100 = **10 V**. These are 10 times the 1 V source (voltage magnification Q) and are equal and opposite in phase.\n\n**Q6.** R = 1 kΩ, C = 0.1 μF.\n(a) f_c = 1/(2πRC) = 1/(2π × 1000 × 10⁻⁷) = 1/(6.283 × 10⁻⁴) = **1.59 kHz**.\n(b) H(f) = 1/(1 + j f/f_c).\nAt f = f_c: |H| = 1/√2 = 0.707, i.e. 20 log₁₀ 0.707 = **-3.01 dB**; phase = -arctan(1) = **-45°**.\nAt f = 10f_c: |H| = 1/√(1 + 100) = 1/10.05 = 0.0995, i.e. **-20.04 dB**; phase = -arctan 10 = **-84.3°**.\n(c) The magnitude is flat (0 dB) below f_c, is -3 dB at f_c and then falls at **-20 dB per decade** (-6 dB per octave); the phase goes from 0° to -90°.\n(d) Swap the positions of R and C, taking the output across the resistor: H = jωRC/(1 + jωRC). With the same R and C the cut-off is the same, 1.59 kHz; it passes high frequencies and rises at +20 dB per decade below f_c.\n\n**Q7.** V_th = 20 V rms, Z_th = 3 + j4 Ω.\nStep 1: maximum power transfer needs Z_L = Z_th* = **3 - j4 Ω** (the reactances cancel).\nStep 2: total resistance 3 + 3 = 6 Ω, so I = 20/6 = 3.33 A.\nStep 3: P = I²R_L = 3.33² × 3 = **33.3 W** (equivalently V_th²/(4R_th) = 400/12 = 33.3 W).\nIf the load must be purely resistive: Step 4: the best R_L = |Z_th| = √(3² + 4²) = 5 Ω.\nStep 5: I = 20/|(3 + 5) + j4| = 20/√(64 + 16) = 20/8.944 = 2.236 A.\nStep 6: P = I²R_L = 2.236² × 5 = **25 W**, less than the 33.3 W of the conjugate-matched load.\n\n**Q8.** Balanced Y load, Z = 20∠30° Ω, V_L = 400 V.\nStep 1: phase voltage V_ph = V_L/√3 = 400/1.732 = **231 V**.\nStep 2: phase (= line) current I = V_ph/|Z| = 231/20 = **11.55 A**.\nStep 3: apparent power S = √3 V_L I_L = 1.732 × 400 × 11.55 = **8.0 kVA** (equivalently 3 × 231 × 11.55).\nStep 4: pf = cos 30° = **0.866 lagging**; P = S cos 30° = 8000 × 0.866 = **6.93 kW**.\nStep 5: Q = S sin 30° = 8000 × 0.5 = **4.0 kvar**.\n\n**Q9.** Ideal transformer, V₁ = 2300 V, V₂ = 230 V, so the turns ratio N₁:N₂ = 10:1.\nStep 1: secondary current I₂ = V₂/R = 230/5 = **46 A**.\nStep 2: primary current I₁ = I₂ × (N₂/N₁) = 46/10 = **4.6 A**.\nStep 3: load power P = V₂I₂ = 230 × 46 = **10.58 kW** (= V₁I₁ = 2300 × 4.6 = 10.58 kW ✓).\nStep 4: reflected impedance = (N₁/N₂)² R = 100 × 5 = **500 Ω** (= V₁/I₁ = 2300/4.6 = 500 ✓).","exam":"# Circuit Analysis I: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** With the passive sign convention, an element absorbs power p = v i when the current\nA) leaves the positive terminal  B) enters the positive terminal  C) is zero  D) is sinusoidal\n\n**2.** KVL states that around any closed loop\nA) the sum of currents is zero  B) the sum of voltages is zero  C) the sum of resistances is zero  D) power is zero\n\n**3.** A 12 A current enters a 3 Ω resistor in parallel with a 6 Ω resistor. The current in the 3 Ω resistor is\nA) 4 A  B) 6 A  C) 8 A  D) 12 A\n\n**4.** A circuit has 5 nodes (including the reference) and no voltage sources. The number of node-voltage equations is\nA) 5  B) 4  C) 3  D) 1\n\n**5.** A supernode is needed when\nA) a resistor is open  B) a voltage source connects two non-reference nodes  C) a current source is in parallel with a resistor  D) the circuit has an op-amp\n\n**6.** The Norton current of a network equals\nA) its open-circuit voltage  B) its short-circuit current  C) V_th × R_th  D) zero\n\n**7.** When maximum power is transferred to the load, the efficiency of the Thevenin equivalent circuit is\nA) 25 percent  B) 50 percent  C) 75 percent  D) 100 percent\n\n**8.** A non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 20 kΩ and R_g = 10 kΩ has a gain of\nA) 2  B) 3  C) -2  D) -3\n\n**9.** For a constant (non-zero) input voltage, the output of an ideal inverting integrator is\nA) a constant  B) a ramp  C) a sinusoid  D) zero\n\n**10.** The energy stored in a 2 H inductor carrying 3 A is\nA) 3 J  B) 6 J  C) 9 J  D) 18 J\n\n**11.** The time constant of a series RL circuit with L = 0.5 H and R = 10 Ω is\nA) 5 s  B) 0.5 s  C) 50 ms  D) 20 ms\n\n**12.** A series RLC circuit is underdamped when\nA) α > ω0  B) α = ω0  C) α < ω0  D) R = 0 only\n\n**13.** The resistance that critically damps a series RLC circuit is\nA) sqrt(L/C)  B) 2 sqrt(L/C)  C) L/C  D) 1/(2 sqrt(LC))\n\n**14.** The impedance of a capacitor C at angular frequency ω is\nA) jωC  B) 1/(jωC)  C) ωC  D) R\n\n**15.** The phasor of v(t) = 10 sin(ωt) V, using cosine as the reference, is\nA) 10∠0°  B) 10∠90°  C) 10∠-90°  D) 10∠180°\n\n**16.** The unit of reactive power is\nA) watt  B) VA  C) var  D) joule\n\n**17.** A capacitive load has a power factor that is\nA) lagging  B) leading  C) always 1  D) always 0\n\n**18.** The quality factor of a series RLC circuit is\nA) ω0 R/L  B) ω0 L/R  C) R/(ω0 L)  D) L/R\n\n**19.** In a balanced Y-connected three-phase system the line voltage is\nA) equal to the phase voltage  B) sqrt 3 times the phase voltage  C) 3 times the phase voltage  D) 1/sqrt 3 times the phase voltage\n\n**20.** An ideal transformer with turns ratio N1:N2 = 10:1 reflects a secondary impedance Z to the primary as\nA) Z/10  B) 10 Z  C) 100 Z  D) Z/100\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** An element has 5 V across it and a current of -2 A flowing into its positive terminal. Is it absorbing or delivering power, and how much?\n\n**B2. (5)** Convert a Δ with R_ab = 30 Ω, R_bc = 60 Ω and R_ca = 30 Ω to an equivalent Y and give the three resistances.\n\n**B3. (5)** An op-amp summing amplifier has R_f = 40 kΩ, R1 = 10 kΩ with v1 = 2 V and R2 = 20 kΩ with v2 = -1 V. Find v_o (supply rails ±12 V).\n\n**B4. (5)** A 12 V source is connected at t = 0 to R = 6 Ω in series with L = 0.3 H (initially zero current). Find i(t), and the current and inductor voltage at t = 50 ms.\n\n**B5. (5)** Find the impedance of a 10 Ω resistor in parallel with a 0.1 H inductor at ω = 100 rad/s, in rectangular and polar form.\n\n**B6. (5)** A load takes 5 A rms from a 120 V rms supply with a current lagging by 36.87°. Find P, Q and S.\n\n**B7. (5)** A series RLC circuit has R = 20 Ω, L = 0.2 H and C = 5 μF. Find ω0, Q and the bandwidth.\n\n**B8. (5)** An RC high-pass filter uses R = 10 kΩ and C = 15.9 nF. Find the cut-off frequency and the gain in dB at 100 Hz.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Nodal analysis with a voltage source.** A 12 V source connects node 1 to ground. A 4 Ω resistor connects node 1 to node 2; 6 Ω connects node 2 to ground; 3 Ω connects node 2 to node 3; 6 Ω connects node 3 to ground; a 2 A current source injects current into node 3.\n(a) Write the node equations for v2 and v3 [4]. (b) Solve [2]. (c) Find the current delivered by the 12 V source and the power of each source [2]. (d) Verify the power balance [2].\n\n**C2. Thevenin with a dependent source.** A 10 V source in series with 5 Ω feeds terminal a; a dependent current source of 0.2 v_a amperes flows from a to ground (v_a is the voltage of a with respect to ground). The load is connected between a and ground.\n(a) Find the open-circuit voltage [3]. (b) Find the short-circuit current [2]. (c) Find R_th and draw the Thevenin equivalent [2]. (d) Find the maximum power the circuit can deliver and the load for it [3].\n\n**C3. Second-order step response.** A series RLC circuit with R = 2 Ω, L = 1 H and C = 0.2 F is connected to a 10 V step at t = 0 with v_C(0) = 2 V and i(0) = 0.\n(a) Find the roots and classify the response [3]. (b) Find v_C(t) [4]. (c) Find the time and value of the first peak, and the overshoot above the final value [3].\n\n**C4. AC power and power-factor correction.** A 230 V rms, 50 Hz supply feeds two loads: load 1 absorbs 3 kW at 0.8 lagging; load 2 absorbs 2 kW at 0.9 leading.\n(a) Find P, Q, S and the power factor of the combination [5]. (b) Find the supply current [2]. (c) Find the capacitor to make the combined power factor unity, and the resulting current [3].\n\n**C5. Active filter.** A first-order active low-pass filter uses an inverting op-amp with an input resistor R = 10 kΩ and a feedback network of R_f = 100 kΩ in parallel with C_f = 1.59 nF.\n(a) Find the DC gain [2]. (b) Find the cut-off frequency [3]. (c) Find the magnitude of the gain at 10 kHz in dB [3]. (d) State the roll-off rate and one advantage over a passive RC filter [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **C** · 11 **C** · 12 **C** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **C** · 16 **C** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **C**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. Passive sign convention: when the current enters the positive terminal the element absorbs power p = vi.\n2. Kirchhoff's voltage law: the algebraic sum of voltages around any closed loop is zero.\n3. Step 1: current divider: I₃ = I × R₆/(R₃ + R₆). Step 2: = 12 × 6/(3 + 6) = 12 × 6/9 = 8 A.\n4. One equation is needed for each non-reference node: 5 - 1 = 4.\n5. A supernode is needed when a voltage source connects two non-reference nodes, because the source current is unknown.\n6. The Norton current is the short-circuit current of the network at the terminals.\n7. At maximum power transfer R_L = R_th, so the same current flows through both and half of the power of the equivalent source goes to the load: 50 percent efficiency.\n8. Step 1: non-inverting gain = 1 + R_f/R_g. Step 2: = 1 + 20/10 = 3.\n9. Step 1: ideal inverting integrator: v_o = -(1/RC) ∫ v_in dt. Step 2: for a constant input the integral grows linearly, so the output is a ramp.\n10. Step 1: w = (1/2)Li². Step 2: = (1/2)(2)(3)² = 9 J.\n11. Step 1: τ = L/R. Step 2: = 0.5/10 = 0.05 s = 50 ms.\n12. Underdamped when the damping rate α = R/2L is less than ω₀ = 1/√(LC) (complex roots).\n13. Step 1: critical damping when R² = 4L/C. Step 2: R = 2√(L/C).\n14. Capacitor impedance Z_C = 1/(jωC).\n15. Step 1: sin(ωt) = cos(ωt - 90°). Step 2: so the cosine-reference phasor is 10∠-90°.\n16. Reactive power is measured in volt-amperes reactive (var).\n17. A capacitive load draws current that leads the voltage, so its power factor is leading.\n18. Quality factor of a series RLC: Q = ω₀L/R.\n19. In a balanced Y system V_line = √3 V_phase.\n20. Step 1: reflected impedance Z' = (N₁/N₂)² Z. Step 2: = 10² Z = 100Z.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1: with the current entering the positive terminal the passive-sign power is p = vi = 5 × (-2) = -10 W.\nStep 2: negative absorbed power means the element delivers power. **It delivers 10 W** (it is a source).\n\n**B2.** Δ resistances R_ab = 30, R_bc = 60, R_ca = 30 Ω. Y legs: each leg = (product of the two adjacent Δ resistors)/(sum of the three).\nStep 1: sum = 30 + 60 + 30 = 120 Ω.\nStep 2: R_a (at node a, between R_ab and R_ca) = (30 × 30)/120 = **7.5 Ω**.\nStep 3: R_b (at node b, between R_ab and R_bc) = (30 × 60)/120 = **15 Ω**.\nStep 4: R_c (at node c, between R_bc and R_ca) = (60 × 30)/120 = **15 Ω**.\n\n**B3.** Step 1: summing amplifier: v_o = -R_f(v₁/R₁ + v₂/R₂).\nStep 2: v₁/R₁ = 2/10 k = 0.2 mA; v₂/R₂ = -1/20 k = -0.05 mA; sum = 0.15 mA.\nStep 3: v_o = -40 k × 0.15 mA = **-6 V** (inside the ±12 V rails).\n\n**B4.** Step 1: τ = L/R = 0.3/6 = 0.05 s.\nStep 2: final current = V/R = 12/6 = 2 A, so **i(t) = 2(1 - e^(-20t)) A**.\nStep 3: at t = 50 ms (one time constant): i = 2(1 - e^(-1)) = 2(0.6321) = **1.264 A**.\nStep 4: inductor voltage v_L = V e^(-t/τ) = 12e^(-1) = **4.41 V**.\nCheck: v_L = 12 - iR = 12 - 6(1.264) = 4.41 V ✓.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: Z_L = jωL = j(100)(0.1) = j10 Ω.\nStep 2: parallel: Z = (10)(j10)/(10 + j10) = j100/(10 + j10).\nStep 3: multiply by the conjugate (10 - j10): j100(10 - j10)/(100 + 100) = (j1000 + 1000)/200 = 5 + j5.\n**Z = 5 + j5 Ω = 7.07∠45° Ω**.\n\n**B6.** Step 1: S = V I = 120 × 5 = **600 VA**.\nStep 2: P = S cos 36.87° = 600 × 0.8 = **480 W**.\nStep 3: Q = S sin 36.87° = 600 × 0.6 = **360 var** (lagging, inductive).\n\n**B7.** R = 20 Ω, L = 0.2 H, C = 5 μF.\nStep 1: ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 1/√(0.2 × 5 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(10⁻⁶) = **1000 rad/s**.\nStep 2: Q = ω₀L/R = (1000)(0.2)/20 = **10**.\nStep 3: BW = ω₀/Q = 1000/10 = **100 rad/s** (15.9 Hz).\n\n**B8.** Step 1: f_c = 1/(2πRC) = 1/(2π × 10⁴ × 15.9 × 10⁻⁹) = 1/(1.0 × 10⁻³) = **1.0 kHz**.\nStep 2: high-pass magnitude |H| = (f/f_c)/√(1 + (f/f_c)²); at 100 Hz, f/f_c = 0.1.\nStep 3: |H| = 0.1/√(1 + 0.01) = 0.1/1.005 = 0.0995.\nStep 4: 20 log₁₀(0.0995) = **-20.0 dB**.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.** Node 1 is held at 12 V; unknowns v₂, v₃.\n(a) Step 1 (KCL node 2): (v₂ - 12)/4 + v₂/6 + (v₂ - v₃)/3 = 0. Collect: v₂(1/4 + 1/6 + 1/3) - v₃/3 = 12/4, i.e. 0.75v₂ - 0.3333v₃ = 3.\nStep 2 (KCL node 3): (v₃ - v₂)/3 + v₃/6 = 2, i.e. -0.3333v₂ + 0.5v₃ = 2.\n(b) Step 3 (solve): multiply the first by 12: 9v₂ - 4v₃ = 36; the second by 6: -2v₂ + 3v₃ = 12.\nStep 4: from the second, v₃ = (12 + 2v₂)/3. Substitute: 9v₂ - 4(12 + 2v₂)/3 = 36, so 27v₂ - 48 - 8v₂ = 108 and 19v₂ = 156.\n**v₂ = 156/19 = 8.21 V**; v₃ = (12 + 16.42)/3 = **180/19 = 9.47 V**.\n(c) Step 5: the 12 V source current into node 2 through the 4 Ω: (12 - 8.21)/4 = **0.947 A**. Power delivered by the 12 V source = 12 × 0.947 = **11.37 W**.\nStep 6: the 2 A source delivers v₃ × 2 = 9.47 × 2 = **18.95 W**.\n(d) Step 7 (absorbed powers): 4 Ω: 0.947² × 4 = 3.59 W; 6 Ω at node 2: 8.21²/6 = 11.24 W; 3 Ω: (8.21 - 9.47)²/3 = 0.53 W; 6 Ω at node 3: 9.47²/6 = 14.96 W. Total = 3.59 + 11.24 + 0.53 + 14.96 = **30.32 W**.\nStep 8: delivered = 11.37 + 18.95 = 30.32 W ✓ (power balance).\n\n**C2.**\n(a) Open circuit (nothing connected at a): the only paths are through the 5 Ω from the 10 V source and the dependent source 0.2v_a to ground.\nStep 1: KCL at a: (v_a - 10)/5 + 0.2v_a = 0.\nStep 2: 0.2v_a - 2 + 0.2v_a = 0, so 0.4v_a = 2 and **V_oc = 5 V**.\n(b) Step 1: short a to ground: v_a = 0, so the dependent source 0.2v_a = 0.\nStep 2: the whole 10 V is across 5 Ω: **I_sc = 10/5 = 2 A**.\n(c) Step 1: R_th = V_oc/I_sc = 5/2 = **2.5 Ω**. The Thevenin equivalent is a 5 V source in series with 2.5 Ω.\n(The dependent source makes R_th differ from the 5 Ω one would get by simply turning off the independent source.)\n(d) Step 1: maximum power at R_L = R_th = **2.5 Ω**.\nStep 2: P_max = V_th²/(4R_th) = 25/(4 × 2.5) = 25/10 = **2.5 W**.\n\n**C3.** R = 2 Ω, L = 1 H, C = 0.2 F, 10 V step, v_C(0) = 2 V, i(0) = 0.\n(a) Step 1: characteristic equation s² + (R/L)s + 1/(LC) = s² + 2s + 5 = 0.\nStep 2: s = [-2 ± √(4 - 20)]/2 = -1 ± j2. Complex roots: **underdamped** (α = 1, ω₀ = √5 = 2.236, ω_d = 2).\n(b) Step 1: v_C = 10 + e^(-t)(A cos 2t + B sin 2t) (final value 10 V).\nStep 2: v_C(0) = 10 + A = 2, so A = -8.\nStep 3: v_C'(0) = i(0)/C = 0. Derivative: v_C' = e^(-t)[(-A + 2B) cos 2t + (-B - 2A) sin 2t], so v_C'(0) = -A + 2B = 0 and B = A/2 = -4.\n**v_C(t) = 10 - e^(-t)(8 cos 2t + 4 sin 2t)**.\n(c) Step 1: differentiate v_C: v_C' = e^(-t)(8 cos 2t + 4 sin 2t) - e^(-t)(-16 sin 2t + 8 cos 2t) = 20e^(-t) sin 2t.\nStep 2: the first zero after t = 0 is where sin 2t = 0, 2t = π: **t = π/2 = 1.571 s**.\nStep 3: v_C(π/2) = 10 - e^(-π/2)(8 cos π + 4 sin π) = 10 - 0.2079(-8) = 10 + 1.663 = **11.66 V**.\nStep 4: overshoot above the final value = 11.66 - 10 = **1.66 V** (16.6 percent of the final value).\n\n**C4.** V = 230 V, f = 50 Hz.\n(a) Step 1 (load 1): P₁ = 3000 W; tan(arccos 0.8) = 0.75, so Q₁ = +2250 var (lagging).\nStep 2 (load 2): P₂ = 2000 W; arccos 0.9 = 25.84°, tan = 0.4843, so Q₂ = -2000(0.4843) = -969 var (leading, capacitive).\nStep 3 (sum): **P = 3000 + 2000 = 5000 W**; **Q = 2250 - 969 = 1281 var**.\nStep 4: S = √(5000² + 1281²) = √(25.0 × 10⁶ + 1.64 × 10⁶) = **5162 VA**.\nStep 5: pf = P/S = 5000/5162 = **0.969 lagging**.\n(b) I = S/V = 5162/230 = **22.4 A**.\n(c) Step 1: to reach unity pf the capacitor must supply the net 1281 var.\nStep 2: C = Q_c/(ωV²) = 1281/(314.16 × 230²) = 1281/(314.16 × 52,900) = 1281/16.62 × 10⁶ = **77 μF**.\nStep 3: new current I = P/V = 5000/230 = **21.7 A**.\n\n**C5.** Inverting active low-pass filter, R = 10 kΩ, R_f = 100 kΩ ∥ C_f = 1.59 nF.\n(a) Step 1: at DC the capacitor is open, so the feedback impedance is R_f and the gain = -R_f/R = -100/10 = **-10** (20 dB magnitude).\n(b) Step 1: f_c = 1/(2πR_fC_f) = 1/(2π × 10⁵ × 1.59 × 10⁻⁹) = 1/(1.0 × 10⁻³) = **1.0 kHz**.\n(c) Step 1: |H| = (R_f/R)/√(1 + (f/f_c)²); at 10 kHz, f/f_c = 10.\nStep 2: |H| = 10/√(1 + 100) = 10/10.05 = 0.995.\nStep 3: 20 log₁₀(0.995) = **-0.04 dB**. (The gain magnitude has fallen from 10 to about 1; relative to the DC gain of 20 dB it is 20 dB lower, as expected a decade above f_c for a first-order filter.)\n(d) The roll-off is **-20 dB per decade** above f_c. Advantages of the active filter: it can provide **gain** and has a **low output impedance**, so a following stage does not change the cut-off frequency (a passive RC filter is loaded by the next stage and has no gain).","notes":"# Circuit Analysis I: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nCircuit analysis is the language of electrical and computer engineering: power supplies, sensors, amplifiers, filters, processors and communication links are all circuits. This course gives you systematic methods to predict voltages and currents in linear circuits. You met the basics in Physics II; here they are developed into complete analysis tools. Conventions: voltages in volts (V), currents in amperes (A), resistance in ohms (Ω), conductance G = 1/R in siemens (S), capacitance in farads (F), inductance in henries (H), j = sqrt(-1), phasors are written in polar form magnitude∠angle.\n\n## Chapter 1. Fundamentals\n\n### 1.1 Charge, current, voltage, power\n- **Current** i = dq/dt (A): the flow of positive charge, with a reference direction shown by an arrow. **Voltage** v_ab = the work per unit charge moving from b to a (V), with the reference polarity marked + and -.\n- **Passive sign convention:** if the current enters the + terminal of an element, then p = v i is the power **absorbed** by the element. If p < 0, the element is delivering power. In any circuit the total absorbed power is zero (conservation of energy). Energy w = ∫ p dt (J). A 100 W lamp used for 10 h uses 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ.\n- **Sources:** an ideal independent voltage source holds its voltage regardless of current; an ideal current source forces its current regardless of voltage. **Dependent (controlled) sources**: VCVS, VCCS, CCVS, CCCS, whose output is proportional to a voltage or current elsewhere in the circuit; they model transistors and amplifiers.\n\n### 1.2 Resistors and Ohm's law\nv = i R (passive convention). Power p = v i = i^2 R = v^2/R. Short circuit R = 0, open circuit R = ∞. Real resistors have a tolerance (for example 5 percent) and a power rating.\n\n### 1.3 Kirchhoff's laws\n- **KCL:** the algebraic sum of currents leaving a node is zero. **KVL:** the algebraic sum of voltages around any closed loop is zero.\n- **Series resistors** carry the same current: R_eq = ΣR; **voltage divider:** v_k = v R_k/ΣR. **Parallel resistors** share the same voltage: G_eq = ΣG; for two, R_eq = R1 R2/(R1 + R2); **current divider:** i_1 = i R2/(R1 + R2).\n- **Example.** 36 V is applied to 4 Ω in series with (6 Ω ∥ 12 Ω) in series with 2 Ω. 6 ∥ 12 = 4 Ω, R_eq = 10 Ω, I = 3.6 A, V across the parallel pair = 14.4 V, branch currents 2.4 A and 1.2 A, total power 129.6 W.\n\n## Chapter 2. Systematic Methods\n\n### 2.1 Node-voltage analysis\nChoose a reference (ground) node. For each other node write KCL in terms of node voltages (using conductances); solve the matrix equation **G v = i_s**. For a circuit with only resistors and independent current sources, G is symmetric with positive diagonal entries (the sum of the conductances at the node) and negative off-diagonal entries (minus the conductance between nodes). Voltage sources: a source between a node and ground fixes that node's voltage; a source between two non-reference nodes forms a **supernode** (write KCL for the combined node plus the constraint equation v_a - v_b = V).\n**Example.** Source 5 A into node 1, 2 A into node 2; R = 2 Ω from node 1 to ground, 4 Ω between nodes 1 and 2, 8 Ω from node 2 to ground:\n(1/2 + 1/4) v1 - (1/4) v2 = 5 and -(1/4) v1 + (1/4 + 1/8) v2 = 2, giving v1 = 76/7 = 10.86 V and v2 = 88/7 = 12.57 V.\n\n### 2.2 Mesh-current analysis\nFor a planar circuit assign a clockwise current to each mesh and write KVL: **R i = v_s**, where the diagonal entries are the total resistance in each mesh and the off-diagonal entries are minus the resistance shared by two meshes. A current source on the boundary of two meshes creates a **supermesh**. **Example.** Mesh 1 has a 10 V source, 2 Ω and the shared 4 Ω; mesh 2 has 6 Ω, the shared 4 Ω and a 4 V source opposing the clockwise current: 6 i1 - 4 i2 = 10; -4 i1 + 10 i2 = -4, so i1 = 1.909 A, i2 = 0.364 A and the current in the shared resistor is i1 - i2 = 1.545 A.\nChoose the method with fewer equations: nodes - 1 versus meshes. Both extend to dependent sources by adding the control variable equation.\n**Dependent-source example.** 1 A into node 1; 2 Ω from node 1 to ground; 4 Ω between node 1 and node 2; 2 Ω from node 2 to ground; a dependent current source 0.5 v1 flowing from node 2 to ground. Node 1: v1/2 + (v1 - v2)/4 = 1; node 2: (v2 - v1)/4 + v2/2 + 0.5 v1 = 0. Solving: v1 = 1.2 V, v2 = -0.4 V.\n\n### 2.3 Linearity, superposition, source transformation\n- **Linearity:** a linear circuit's response is proportional to the source scaling (homogeneity) and sums (additivity).\n- **Superposition:** the response to several independent sources is the sum of the responses to each acting alone, with the others turned off (voltage sources replaced by short circuits, current sources by open circuits). Dependent sources always remain. It does not apply to power.\n- **Source transformation:** a voltage source V in series with R is equivalent to a current source I = V/R in parallel with R. It simplifies series-parallel networks.\n\n### 2.4 Thevenin and Norton equivalents\nAny linear two-terminal network is equivalent to a voltage source V_th in series with R_th (Thevenin), or a current source I_N = V_th/R_th in parallel with R_th (Norton). V_th is the open-circuit voltage; I_N is the short-circuit current; R_th = V_th/I_N, or the resistance seen with independent sources turned off. With dependent sources, find R_th as V_test/I_test (or V_oc/I_sc).\n**Example.** 12 V source and 6 Ω in series feeding a node a; 3 Ω from a to ground; a 2 A source into node a: (v_a - 12)/6 + v_a/3 = 2 gives V_th = 8 V; R_th = 6 ∥ 3 = 2 Ω; I_N = 4 A.\n**Maximum power transfer:** a load R_L receives the most power when R_L = R_th: P_max = V_th^2/(4 R_th) (here 8 W). The efficiency at maximum power is only 50 percent, so power systems do not operate there.\n\n## Chapter 3. Operational Amplifiers\n\nAn op-amp is a high-gain differential amplifier with inputs v+ and v- and output v_o = A (v+ - v-), A ~ 10^5 or more. **Ideal model:** infinite gain, infinite input resistance, zero output resistance. With **negative feedback** this gives the **virtual short**: v+ = v- and i+ = i- = 0. The output is limited by the supply rails (saturation, for example ±12 V).\n- **Inverting amplifier:** v_o = -(R_f/R_in) v_in. R_f = 100 kΩ, R_in = 10 kΩ gives gain -10: 0.5 V in produces -5 V; 1.5 V in would give -15 V, so the output saturates at -12 V.\n- **Non-inverting amplifier:** v_o = (1 + R_f/R_g) v_in. **Voltage follower** (gain 1): a buffer with very high input resistance.\n- **Summing amplifier:** v_o = -R_f (v1/R1 + v2/R2 + ...). **Difference amplifier:** v_o = (R2/R1)(v2 - v1) with matched resistors. **Integrator:** v_o = -(1/RC) ∫ v_in dt. **Differentiator:** v_o = -RC dv_in/dt.\n- Analysis steps: assume the virtual short, write KCL at the inverting node, solve. Practical limits: gain-bandwidth product, slew rate, offset and bias currents, output swing.\n\n## Chapter 4. Energy-Storage Elements\n\n### 4.1 Capacitors and inductors\n- **Capacitor:** i = C dv/dt; energy w = (1/2) C v^2. The voltage cannot change instantaneously. In DC steady state it acts as an open circuit. Parallel: C_eq = ΣC; series: 1/C_eq = Σ1/C.\n- **Inductor:** v = L di/dt; w = (1/2) L i^2. The current cannot change instantaneously. In DC steady state it acts as a short circuit. Series: L_eq = ΣL; parallel: 1/L_eq = Σ1/L. Mutual inductance couples two coils: v_2 = M di_1/dt.\n- **Example.** A 2 μF capacitor with v(t) = 5t^2 volts has i = C dv/dt = 20t μA. A 0.5 H inductor with i = 4 A stores 4 J.\n\n### 4.2 First-order circuits\nAny circuit with one energy-storage element and resistors reduces to its Thevenin equivalent (V_th, R_th) driving that element, so the response has the form\n**x(t) = x(∞) + [x(0+) - x(∞)] e^(-t/τ)**, with **τ = R_th C** (RC) or **τ = L/R_th** (RL).\n- **Initial value** x(0+): use continuity (v_C and i_L cannot jump). **Final value** x(∞): DC steady state (capacitors open, inductors shorted). **Time constant** τ: the time to cover 63.2 percent of the change; after 5τ the transient is within 1 percent.\n- **RC example:** R = 2 kΩ, C = 500 μF (τ = 1 s), source 10 V, v_C(0) = 2 V: v_C = 10 - 8 e^(-t); it reaches 9 V at t = ln 8 = 2.08 s.\n- **RL example:** V = 20 V, R = 10 Ω, L = 0.2 H: τ = 20 ms, i = 2(1 - e^(-50t)) A, final energy (1/2)(0.2)(4) = 0.4 J.\n- **Switched circuits:** analyse before the switch (to get the initial value), then after (to get final value and τ). Step and pulse inputs are handled by repeating the method over each interval. **Op-amp integrators** use the same RC relation.\n\n### 4.3 Second-order circuits\nA circuit with two storage elements gives a second-order equation. For the **series RLC** circuit with a step source V: L C v_C'' + R C v_C' + v_C = V. The characteristic equation is s^2 + (R/L)s + 1/(LC) = 0 with the damping coefficient α = R/(2L) and the natural frequency ω0 = 1/sqrt(LC).\n- **Overdamped** (α > ω0): two real negative roots, v = V + A1 e^(s1 t) + A2 e^(s2 t). R = 6, L = 1, C = 1/8: s = -2, -4.\n- **Critically damped** (α = ω0): v = V + (A1 + A2 t) e^(-αt). R = 4, L = 1, C = 1/4: s = -2 twice.\n- **Underdamped** (α < ω0): s = -α ± jω_d with ω_d = sqrt(ω0^2 - α^2), v = V + e^(-αt)(A1 cos ω_d t + A2 sin ω_d t). R = 4, L = 1, C = 1/5: s = -2 ± j1; with zero initial energy and V = 10 V: v_C = 10 - e^(-2t)(10 cos t + 20 sin t).\n- **Parallel RLC:** α = 1/(2RC), the same ω0. Initial conditions come from v_C(0+) and i_L(0+) (and v_C' = i_C/C). The same mathematics governs mass-spring-damper systems.\n\n## Chapter 5. Sinusoidal Steady State and Phasors\n\n### 5.1 Phasors and impedance\nA sinusoid v(t) = V_m cos(ωt + φ) is represented by the phasor V = V_m∠φ (peak) or V_rms = V_m/sqrt 2. Differentiation becomes multiplication by jω, so differential equations become algebraic.\n- **Impedance** Z = V/I (Ω): resistor Z = R; inductor Z = jωL; capacitor Z = 1/(jωC) = -j/(ωC). Reactance X (the imaginary part): X_L = ωL, X_C = -1/(ωC). **Admittance** Y = 1/Z.\n- Series and parallel combination, voltage and current dividers, node and mesh analysis, superposition (for sources at different frequencies superpose in the time domain), Thevenin and Norton all work with complex numbers.\n- **Example.** 40 mH in series with 30 Ω at ω = 1000 rad/s: Z = 30 + j40 = 50∠53.13° Ω; for V = 100∠0° V, I = 2∠-53.13° A (current lags voltage). The sum of 10∠30° and 5∠-60° is 11.18∠3.4°.\n\n### 5.2 AC power\nWith v = V_m cos(ωt + φ_v) and i = I_m cos(ωt + φ_i), define the phase θ = φ_v - φ_i (the impedance angle).\n- **Average (real) power** P = V_rms I_rms cos θ (W). **Reactive power** Q = V_rms I_rms sin θ (var, positive for inductive loads). **Apparent power** S = V_rms I_rms (VA). **Complex power** S = V I* = P + jQ; |S|^2 = P^2 + Q^2. **Power factor** pf = cos θ (lagging for inductive, leading for capacitive).\n- For a resistor P = I_rms^2 R; pure inductors and capacitors absorb no average power (they exchange it).\n- **Power-factor correction:** a load of 5 kW at 230 V rms, pf 0.8 lagging (S = 6.25 kVA, Q = 3.75 kvar, I = 27.2 A) is corrected to pf 0.95 by a parallel capacitor supplying Q_c = Q - P tan(arccos 0.95) = 3750 - 1644 = 2106 var, so C = Q_c/(ω V^2) = 127 μF at 50 Hz. The line current drops to 22.9 A, which reduces losses.\n- **Maximum power transfer in AC:** the load impedance should be the complex conjugate of the Thevenin impedance: Z_L = Z_th*. P_max = |V_th|^2/(8 R_th) with peak values (|V_th|^2/(4 R_th) with rms values).\n\n### 5.3 Frequency response and resonance\n- **Transfer function** H(jω) = V_out/V_in. A decibel gain is 20 log10|H|. **Bode plots** show |H| (dB) and the phase against log frequency. A first-order low-pass H = 1/(1 + jω/ω_c) with ω_c = 1/RC has -3 dB and -45° at the cut-off, then falls 20 dB per decade. R = 1 kΩ, C = 0.1 μF gives f_c = 1.59 kHz.\n- **Series RLC resonance** at ω0 = 1/sqrt(LC): the reactances cancel, |Z| = R is minimum and the current is maximum. **Quality factor** Q = ω0 L/R = 1/(ω0 R C); bandwidth BW = ω0/Q. R = 10 Ω, L = 10 mH, C = 1 μF: ω0 = 10^4 rad/s (1.59 kHz), Q = 10, BW = 1000 rad/s. At resonance the inductor and capacitor voltages are Q times the source voltage. **Parallel resonance** gives maximum impedance.\n- **Filters:** low-pass, high-pass, band-pass and band-stop circuits made from RC, RL and RLC networks, and active filters with op-amps.\n\n### 5.4 Three-phase systems and transformers (introduction)\nA balanced **three-phase** source has three voltages of equal magnitude 120° apart. In a Y connection the line voltage is sqrt 3 times the phase voltage. For a balanced load, total real power P = sqrt 3 V_L I_L cos θ. Example: V_L = 400 V, a Y-connected load Z = 20∠30° Ω per phase: V_phase = 231 V, I = 11.5 A, P = 6.93 kW. Three-phase power delivers constant total instantaneous power and uses less copper than single-phase. An **ideal transformer** has V2/V1 = N2/N1 and I2/I1 = N1/N2, and transforms impedances by (N1/N2)^2.\n\n## Chapter 6. Problem-Solving Strategy and Checks\n1. Redraw the circuit, label nodes and reference directions, and note what is asked.\n2. Pick the method that gives the fewest equations (node, mesh, source transformation, Thevenin).\n3. Solve, then **check**: KCL at a node, KVL on a loop, power balance (supplied equals absorbed), limiting cases (R → 0 or ∞), units.\n4. Verify with a simulator (LTspice, Falstad, ngspice) once you have a hand solution.\n\n## Chapter 7. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Inconsistent sign conventions for current direction and voltage polarity (and the passive sign convention for power).\n2. Turning off sources incorrectly in superposition and Thevenin (voltage sources become shorts, current sources become opens) or turning off dependent sources.\n3. Forgetting to include the supernode constraint, or double counting the shared resistor in mesh analysis.\n4. Treating a capacitor voltage or inductor current as discontinuous at a switching instant.\n5. Using peak values where rms is needed (power) and degrees versus radians in phasor arithmetic.\n6. Taking the wrong sign for the reactance of a capacitor (X_C is negative) or the sign of reactive power.\n7. Adding impedances in parallel as if they were resistances without using complex arithmetic.\n8. Forgetting that the ideal op-amp analysis requires negative feedback (virtual short) and checking for saturation.\n","plan":"# Circuit Analysis I: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 5 to 6 problems, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Circuit analysis is a problem-solving skill: aim for 10 or more complete circuits per week, solved by hand and then checked in a simulator. Install a free simulator now: LTspice, ngspice (with KiCad or Qucs-S) or the browser-based Falstad circuit simulator.\n\n## Textbooks\n- James Nilsson and Susan Riedel, *Electric Circuits* (Pearson). The standard text.\n- Charles Alexander and Matthew Sadiku, *Fundamentals of Electric Circuits*. Many worked examples.\n- Agarwal and Lang, *Foundations of Analog and Digital Electronic Circuits*.\n- Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill, *The Art of Electronics* (read for intuition; the practical reference for later courses).\n- Ulaby and Maharbiz, *Circuits* (free sample chapters online) for an alternative explanation of phasors.\n\n## Free courses\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.002 Circuits and Electronics and 6.003 Signals and Systems (for later).\n- Khan Academy Electrical Engineering; The Organic Chemistry Tutor and Eugene Khutoryansky videos for visual explanations.\n- All About Circuits (free textbook-style site) and the EEVblog fundamentals series.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Basics: charge, current, voltage, power, sources, Ohm's law (Ch 1.1, 1.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Checkpoint: determine whether an element absorbs or delivers power.\n\n**Week 2: Kirchhoff's laws, dividers, series/parallel reduction (Ch 1.3).** Practise reducing 20 resistor networks. Habit: always do a power-balance check.\n\n**Week 3: Node-voltage method (Ch 2.1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2. Include voltage sources (supernodes).\n\n**Week 4: Mesh-current method and dependent sources (Ch 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3, Q5. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Linearity, superposition, source transformation, Thevenin and Norton, maximum power (Ch 2.3, 2.4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q7.\n\n**Week 6: Operational amplifiers (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6. Build an inverting amplifier in a simulator and observe saturation.\n\n**Week 7: Capacitors and inductors (Ch 4.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1, Q7. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: First-order transients (Ch 4.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2 to Q5, Q8. Simulate each with a transient analysis and compare with your equations.\n\n**Week 9: Second-order circuits (Ch 4.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q6. Vary R in a simulator to see overdamped, critical and underdamped step responses.\n\n**Week 10: Phasors, impedance and AC analysis (Ch 5.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1 to Q3. Practise complex arithmetic by hand and with a calculator.\n\n**Week 11: AC power and power-factor correction (Ch 5.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4, Q7.\n\n**Week 12: Frequency response, resonance and filters (Ch 5.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5, Q6. Plot Bode diagrams with Python (scipy.signal) and in a simulator.\n\n**Week 13: Three-phase systems and transformers (Ch 5.4).** Do: Assignment 3 Q8, Q9.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can analyse resistive circuits with node-voltage and mesh-current methods, including supernodes and dependent sources.\n- [ ] I can find Thevenin and Norton equivalents and apply maximum power transfer.\n- [ ] I can analyse ideal op-amp circuits (inverting, non-inverting, summing, difference, integrator).\n- [ ] I can solve first-order RC and RL circuits using initial value, final value and time constant.\n- [ ] I can solve series and parallel RLC step responses and classify the damping.\n- [ ] I can analyse AC circuits with phasors and impedances.\n- [ ] I can compute real, reactive, apparent and complex power and correct the power factor.\n- [ ] I can find resonance, Q and the frequency response of RC and RLC circuits.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Label everything (nodes, currents, polarities) before writing a single equation.\n2. Always verify with at least one independent check: power balance, KCL at another node, or a simulator.\n3. Keep a list of standard results (divider formulas, time-constant formulas, phasor impedances).\n4. Estimate before computing: guess the order of magnitude and sign of each answer.\n5. Redo each assignment problem a week later with the solution covered.\n","test":"# Circuit Analysis I: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The power dissipated when 12 V is applied across a 6 Ω resistor is\nA) 2 W  B) 12 W  C) 24 W  D) 72 W\n\n**2.** Resistors of 4 Ω and 6 Ω in series have an equivalent resistance of\nA) 2.4 Ω  B) 10 Ω  C) 24 Ω  D) 5 Ω\n\n**3.** Resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel have an equivalent resistance of\nA) 9 Ω  B) 4.5 Ω  C) 2 Ω  D) 18 Ω\n\n**4.** Kirchhoff's current law states that\nA) the sum of voltages around a loop is zero  B) the sum of currents leaving a node is zero  C) current is proportional to voltage  D) power is conserved only in loops\n\n**5.** 12 V is applied across 2 kΩ in series with 4 kΩ. The voltage across the 4 kΩ resistor is\nA) 4 V  B) 6 V  C) 8 V  D) 12 V\n\n**6.** The Thevenin resistance of a network is found by\nA) shorting the load and measuring current  B) turning off independent sources and finding the resistance seen at the terminals  C) adding all resistors  D) opening all sources\n\n**7.** Maximum power is transferred to a load when\nA) R_L = 0  B) R_L = R_th  C) R_L is very large  D) R_L = 2 R_th\n\n**8.** In superposition, an independent voltage source that is turned off is replaced by\nA) an open circuit  B) a short circuit  C) a resistor  D) a current source\n\n**9.** An inverting amplifier has R_f = 50 kΩ and R_in = 10 kΩ. Its voltage gain is\nA) 5  B) -5  C) 6  D) -6\n\n**10.** The \"virtual short\" of an ideal op-amp with negative feedback means\nA) v+ = v- (the inputs are at the same voltage)  B) the output is zero  C) the supply is shorted  D) the input current is large\n\n**11.** In DC steady state a capacitor behaves like\nA) a short circuit  B) an open circuit  C) a resistor  D) a voltage source\n\n**12.** Which quantity cannot change instantaneously?\nA) capacitor current  B) inductor current  C) resistor voltage  D) inductor voltage\n\n**13.** The time constant of 10 kΩ in series with 10 μF is\nA) 1 ms  B) 10 ms  C) 0.1 s  D) 100 s\n\n**14.** After 5 time constants an RC charging transient has completed about\nA) 63 percent  B) 86 percent  C) 95 percent  D) 99 percent\n\n**15.** The energy stored in a 4 μF capacitor at 10 V is\nA) 0.2 mJ  B) 2 mJ  C) 40 μJ  D) 0.4 J\n\n**16.** At ω = 1000 rad/s the impedance of a 1 μF capacitor is\nA) j1000 Ω  B) -j1000 Ω  C) 1000 Ω  D) -j0.001 Ω\n\n**17.** At ω = 100 rad/s the impedance of a 0.1 H inductor is\nA) j10 Ω  B) -j10 Ω  C) 10 Ω  D) j0.1 Ω\n\n**18.** A sinusoid with a peak value of 10 V has an rms value of\nA) 5 V  B) 7.07 V  C) 10 V  D) 14.1 V\n\n**19.** A load with R = 3 Ω and X_L = 4 Ω has a power factor of\nA) 0.6  B) 0.75  C) 0.8  D) 1.33\n\n**20.** The resonant frequency (in rad/s) of L = 1 mH and C = 1 μF is about\nA) 1000  B) 31,600  C) 10^6  D) 159\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** A 6 A source feeds node 1. A 3 Ω resistor connects node 1 to ground, a 6 Ω resistor connects nodes 1 and 2, and a 3 Ω resistor connects node 2 to ground. (a) Write the node-voltage equations and find v1 and v2 [6]. (b) Find the power delivered by the source and verify it equals the power absorbed by the resistors [4].\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** A 10 V source in series with 5 Ω drives node a; 20 Ω connects a to ground. The load is connected between a and ground. (a) Find the Thevenin and Norton equivalents [4]. (b) Find the load voltage and power for R_L = 12 Ω [3]. (c) Find the maximum power and the load that receives it [3].\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** A 12 V source charges a 50 μF capacitor through 2 kΩ; the capacitor is initially uncharged. (a) Find τ and v_C(t) [3]. (b) Find the time to reach 6 V [2]. (c) Find v_C at t = 0.2 s [2]. (d) Find the initial current and the final stored energy [3].\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** A series circuit has R = 30 Ω and L = 0.1 H connected to 100 V rms at ω = 400 rad/s. (a) Find the impedance and the current phasor [4]. (b) Find P, Q, S and the power factor [3]. (c) Find the parallel capacitor that makes the power factor unity [3].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **C** · 2 **B** · 3 **C** · 4 **B** · 5 **C** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **C** · 14 **D** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **A** · 18 **B** · 19 **A** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons:\n1. Step 1: P = V²/R. Step 2: = 12²/6 = 144/6 = 24 W.\n2. Step 1: series resistances add. Step 2: 4 + 6 = 10 Ω.\n3. Step 1: parallel: R = (R₁R₂)/(R₁ + R₂). Step 2: (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 18/9 = 2 Ω.\n4. KCL: the algebraic sum of currents leaving (or entering) a node is zero (the loop law is KVL).\n5. Step 1: voltage divider: V = 12 × 4k/(2k + 4k). Step 2: = 12 × 4/6 = 8 V.\n6. The Thevenin resistance is found by turning off independent sources (voltage sources shorted, current sources opened) and finding the resistance seen at the terminals.\n7. Maximum power transfer occurs when R_L = R_th.\n8. A turned-off ideal voltage source has zero volts across it, which is a short circuit (a turned-off current source is an open circuit).\n9. Step 1: inverting amplifier gain = -R_f/R_in. Step 2: = -50/10 = -5.\n10. With negative feedback and high gain the input voltage difference is driven to zero: v₊ = v₋ (virtual short).\n11. In DC steady state dv/dt = 0, so i = C dv/dt = 0: the capacitor acts like an open circuit.\n12. The inductor current cannot change instantaneously (v = L di/dt would be infinite); the capacitor voltage also cannot jump, but capacitor and inductor voltages/currents of the other kind can.\n13. Step 1: τ = RC. Step 2: = (10 × 10³)(10 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.1 s.\n14. Step 1: fraction complete = 1 - e^(-5) = 1 - 0.0067. Step 2: = 0.9933, about 99 percent.\n15. Step 1: w = (1/2)CV². Step 2: = (1/2)(4 × 10⁻⁶)(10)² = (2 × 10⁻⁶)(100) = 2 × 10⁻⁴ J = 0.2 mJ.\n16. Step 1: Z_C = 1/(jωC). Step 2: = 1/(j × 1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/(j0.001) = -j1000 Ω.\n17. Step 1: Z_L = jωL. Step 2: = j(100)(0.1) = j10 Ω.\n18. Step 1: V_rms = V_peak/√2. Step 2: = 10/1.414 = 7.07 V.\n19. Step 1: |Z| = √(3² + 4²) = 5 Ω. Step 2: pf = R/|Z| = 3/5 = 0.6.\n20. Step 1: ω₀ = 1/√(LC). Step 2: = 1/√(10⁻³ × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(10⁻⁹) = 31,623 rad/s.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1 (KCL node 1): v₁/3 + (v₁ - v₂)/6 = 6.\nStep 2 (KCL node 2): (v₂ - v₁)/6 + v₂/3 = 0. Multiply by 6: (v₂ - v₁) + 2v₂ = 0, so -v₁ + 3v₂ = 0 and **v₂ = v₁/3**.\nStep 3: substitute into node 1: v₁/3 + (v₁ - v₁/3)/6 = v₁/3 + (2v₁/3)/6 = v₁/3 + v₁/9 = 4v₁/9 = 6.\nStep 4: **v₁ = 6 × 9/4 = 13.5 V**, and **v₂ = 13.5/3 = 4.5 V**.\n(b) Step 1: the source delivers P = v₁ × 6 = 13.5 × 6 = **81 W**.\nStep 2 (resistor powers, P = v²/R): 3 Ω at node 1: 13.5²/3 = 60.75 W; 6 Ω: (13.5 - 4.5)²/6 = 81/6 = 13.5 W; 3 Ω at node 2: 4.5²/3 = 6.75 W.\nStep 3: total = 60.75 + 13.5 + 6.75 = **81 W** ✓ equals the source power.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Step 1: V_th is the open-circuit voltage at a: divider 20/(5 + 20) × 10 = 0.8 × 10 = **8 V**.\nStep 2: R_th with the source shorted: 5 ∥ 20 = (5 × 20)/(25) = **4 Ω**.\nStep 3: Norton current I_N = V_th/R_th = 8/4 = **2 A**. Check: short-circuit current = 10/5 = 2 A ✓.\n(b) Step 1: V_L = V_th R_L/(R_th + R_L) = 8 × 12/(4 + 12) = 8 × 0.75 = **6 V**.\nStep 2: P = V_L²/R_L = 36/12 = **3 W**.\n(c) Step 1: maximum power when R_L = R_th = **4 Ω**.\nStep 2: P_max = V_th²/(4R_th) = 64/16 = **4 W** (V_L = 4 V, I = 1 A, P = 4 × 1 ✓).\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Step 1: τ = RC = (2000)(50 × 10⁻⁶) = **0.1 s**.\nStep 2: uncharged capacitor charging to 12 V: **v_C(t) = 12(1 - e^(-t/0.1)) = 12(1 - e^(-10t)) V**.\n(b) Step 1: set 6 = 12(1 - e^(-10t)): 1 - e^(-10t) = 0.5, so e^(-10t) = 0.5.\nStep 2: t = ln 2/10 = 0.6931/10 = **0.0693 s**.\n(c) Step 1: at t = 0.2 s: e^(-10 × 0.2) = e^(-2) = 0.1353.\nStep 2: v_C = 12(1 - 0.1353) = **10.38 V**.\n(d) Step 1: initial current: I(0⁺) = V/R = 12/2000 = **6 mA** (the capacitor is a short at t = 0).\nStep 2: final stored energy: w = (1/2)CV² = (1/2)(50 × 10⁻⁶)(12)² = (25 × 10⁻⁶)(144) = **3.6 mJ**.\n\n**B4.** R = 30 Ω, L = 0.1 H, V = 100 V rms, ω = 400 rad/s.\n(a) Step 1: X_L = ωL = 400 × 0.1 = 40 Ω, so Z = 30 + j40.\nStep 2: |Z| = √(30² + 40²) = 50 Ω, angle = arctan(40/30) = 53.13°. **Z = 50∠53.13° Ω**.\nStep 3: I = V/Z = 100∠0°/50∠53.13° = **2∠-53.13° A** (lagging).\n(b) Step 1: P = I²R = 2² × 30 = **120 W**.\nStep 2: Q = I²X = 4 × 40 = **160 var**.\nStep 3: S = VI = 100 × 2 = **200 VA**.\nStep 4: pf = P/S = 120/200 = **0.6 lagging**.\n(c) Step 1: unity power factor needs the capacitor to supply all 160 var.\nStep 2: Q_c = ωCV², so C = Q_c/(ωV²) = 160/(400 × 100²) = 160/4,000,000 = **40 μF**."},mcq:[{"q":"The power dissipated when 12 V is applied across a 6 Ω resistor is","options":["2 W","12 W","24 W","72 W"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: P = V²/R. Step 2: = 12²/6 = 144/6 = 24 W.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Resistors of 4 Ω and 6 Ω in series have an equivalent resistance of","options":["2.4 Ω","10 Ω","24 Ω","5 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: series resistances add. Step 2: 4 + 6 = 10 Ω.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Resistors of 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel have an equivalent resistance of","options":["9 Ω","4.5 Ω","2 Ω","18 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: parallel: R = (R₁R₂)/(R₁ + R₂). Step 2: (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 18/9 = 2 Ω.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Kirchhoff's current law states that","options":["the sum of voltages around a loop is zero","the sum of currents leaving a node is zero","current is proportional to voltage","power is conserved only in loops"],"answer":1,"explain":"KCL: the algebraic sum of currents leaving (or entering) a node is zero (the loop law is KVL).","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"12 V is applied across 2 kΩ in series with 4 kΩ. The voltage across the 4 kΩ resistor is","options":["4 V","6 V","8 V","12 V"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: voltage divider: V = 12 × 4k/(2k + 4k). Step 2: = 12 × 4/6 = 8 V.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Thevenin resistance of a network is found by","options":["shorting the load and measuring current","turning off independent sources and finding the resistance seen at the terminals","adding all resistors","opening all sources"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Thevenin resistance is found by turning off independent sources (voltage sources shorted, current sources opened) and finding the resistance seen at the terminals.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Maximum power is transferred to a load when","options":["R_L = 0","R_L = R_th","R_L is very large","R_L = 2 R_th"],"answer":1,"explain":"Maximum power transfer occurs when R_L = R_th.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In superposition, an independent voltage source that is turned off is replaced by","options":["an open circuit","a short circuit","a resistor","a current source"],"answer":1,"explain":"A turned-off ideal voltage source has zero volts across it, which is a short circuit (a turned-off current source is an open circuit).","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An inverting amplifier has R_f = 50 kΩ and R_in = 10 kΩ. Its voltage gain is","options":["5","-5","6","-6"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: inverting amplifier gain = -R_f/R_in. Step 2: = -50/10 = -5.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The \"virtual short\" of an ideal op-amp with negative feedback means","options":["v+ = v- (the inputs are at the same voltage)","the output is zero","the supply is shorted","the input current is large"],"answer":0,"explain":"With negative feedback and high gain the input voltage difference is driven to zero: v₊ = v₋ (virtual short).","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In DC steady state a capacitor behaves like","options":["a short circuit","an open circuit","a resistor","a voltage source"],"answer":1,"explain":"In DC steady state dv/dt = 0, so i = C dv/dt = 0: the capacitor acts like an open circuit.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which quantity cannot change instantaneously?","options":["capacitor current","inductor current","resistor voltage","inductor voltage"],"answer":1,"explain":"The inductor current cannot change instantaneously (v = L di/dt would be infinite); the capacitor voltage also cannot jump, but capacitor and inductor voltages/currents of the other kind can.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The time constant of 10 kΩ in series with 10 μF is","options":["1 ms","10 ms","0.1 s","100 s"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: τ = RC. Step 2: = (10 × 10³)(10 × 10⁻⁶) = 0.1 s.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"After 5 time constants an RC charging transient has completed about","options":["63 percent","86 percent","95 percent","99 percent"],"answer":3,"explain":"Step 1: fraction complete = 1 - e^(-5) = 1 - 0.0067. Step 2: = 0.9933, about 99 percent.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The energy stored in a 4 μF capacitor at 10 V is","options":["0.2 mJ","2 mJ","40 μJ","0.4 J"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: w = (1/2)CV². Step 2: = (1/2)(4 × 10⁻⁶)(10)² = (2 × 10⁻⁶)(100) = 2 × 10⁻⁴ J = 0.2 mJ.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"At ω = 1000 rad/s the impedance of a 1 μF capacitor is","options":["j1000 Ω","-j1000 Ω","1000 Ω","-j0.001 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: Z_C = 1/(jωC). Step 2: = 1/(j × 1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 1/(j0.001) = -j1000 Ω.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"At ω = 100 rad/s the impedance of a 0.1 H inductor is","options":["j10 Ω","-j10 Ω","10 Ω","j0.1 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: Z_L = jωL. Step 2: = j(100)(0.1) = j10 Ω.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A sinusoid with a peak value of 10 V has an rms value of","options":["5 V","7.07 V","10 V","14.1 V"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: V_rms = V_peak/√2. Step 2: = 10/1.414 = 7.07 V.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A load with R = 3 Ω and X_L = 4 Ω has a power factor of","options":["0.6","0.75","0.8","1.33"],"answer":0,"explain":"Step 1: |Z| = √(3² + 4²) = 5 Ω. Step 2: pf = R/|Z| = 3/5 = 0.6.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The resonant frequency (in rad/s) of L = 1 mH and C = 1 μF is about","options":["1000","31,600","10^6","159"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: ω₀ = 1/√(LC). Step 2: = 1/√(10⁻³ × 10⁻⁶) = 1/√(10⁻⁹) = 31,623 rad/s.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With the passive sign convention, an element absorbs power p = v i when the current","options":["leaves the positive terminal","enters the positive terminal","is zero","is sinusoidal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Passive sign convention: when the current enters the positive terminal the element absorbs power p = vi.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"KVL states that around any closed loop","options":["the sum of currents is zero","the sum of voltages is zero","the sum of resistances is zero","power is zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kirchhoff's voltage law: the algebraic sum of voltages around any closed loop is zero.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A 12 A current enters a 3 Ω resistor in parallel with a 6 Ω resistor. The current in the 3 Ω resistor is","options":["4 A","6 A","8 A","12 A"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: current divider: I₃ = I × R₆/(R₃ + R₆). Step 2: = 12 × 6/(3 + 6) = 12 × 6/9 = 8 A.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A circuit has 5 nodes (including the reference) and no voltage sources. The number of node-voltage equations is","options":["5","4","3","1"],"answer":1,"explain":"One equation is needed for each non-reference node: 5 - 1 = 4.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A supernode is needed when","options":["a resistor is open","a voltage source connects two non-reference nodes","a current source is in parallel with a resistor","the circuit has an op-amp"],"answer":1,"explain":"A supernode is needed when a voltage source connects two non-reference nodes, because the source current is unknown.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The Norton current of a network equals","options":["its open-circuit voltage","its short-circuit current","V_th × R_th","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Norton current is the short-circuit current of the network at the terminals.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"When maximum power is transferred to the load, the efficiency of the Thevenin equivalent circuit is","options":["25 percent","50 percent","75 percent","100 percent"],"answer":1,"explain":"At maximum power transfer R_L = R_th, so the same current flows through both and half of the power of the equivalent source goes to the load: 50 percent efficiency.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A non-inverting amplifier with R_f = 20 kΩ and R_g = 10 kΩ has a gain of","options":["2","3","-2","-3"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: non-inverting gain = 1 + R_f/R_g. Step 2: = 1 + 20/10 = 3.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"For a constant (non-zero) input voltage, the output of an ideal inverting integrator is","options":["a constant","a ramp","a sinusoid","zero"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: ideal inverting integrator: v_o = -(1/RC) ∫ v_in dt. Step 2: for a constant input the integral grows linearly, so the output is a ramp.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The energy stored in a 2 H inductor carrying 3 A is","options":["3 J","6 J","9 J","18 J"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: w = (1/2)Li². Step 2: = (1/2)(2)(3)² = 9 J.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The time constant of a series RL circuit with L = 0.5 H and R = 10 Ω is","options":["5 s","0.5 s","50 ms","20 ms"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: τ = L/R. Step 2: = 0.5/10 = 0.05 s = 50 ms.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A series RLC circuit is underdamped when","options":["α > ω0","α = ω0","α < ω0","R = 0 only"],"answer":2,"explain":"Underdamped when the damping rate α = R/2L is less than ω₀ = 1/√(LC) (complex roots).","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The resistance that critically damps a series RLC circuit is","options":["sqrt(L/C)","2 sqrt(L/C)","L/C","1/(2 sqrt(LC))"],"answer":1,"explain":"Step 1: critical damping when R² = 4L/C. Step 2: R = 2√(L/C).","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The impedance of a capacitor C at angular frequency ω is","options":["jωC","1/(jωC)","ωC","R"],"answer":1,"explain":"Capacitor impedance Z_C = 1/(jωC).","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The phasor of v(t) = 10 sin(ωt) V, using cosine as the reference, is","options":["10∠0°","10∠90°","10∠-90°","10∠180°"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: sin(ωt) = cos(ωt - 90°). Step 2: so the cosine-reference phasor is 10∠-90°.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The unit of reactive power is","options":["watt","VA","var","joule"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reactive power is measured in volt-amperes reactive (var).","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A capacitive load has a power factor that is","options":["lagging","leading","always 1","always 0"],"answer":1,"explain":"A capacitive load draws current that leads the voltage, so its power factor is leading.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The quality factor of a series RLC circuit is","options":["ω0 R/L","ω0 L/R","R/(ω0 L)","L/R"],"answer":1,"explain":"Quality factor of a series RLC: Q = ω₀L/R.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a balanced Y-connected three-phase system the line voltage is","options":["equal to the phase voltage","sqrt 3 times the phase voltage","3 times the phase voltage","1/sqrt 3 times the phase voltage"],"answer":1,"explain":"In a balanced Y system V_line = √3 V_phase.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"An ideal transformer with turns ratio N1:N2 = 10:1 reflects a secondary impedance Z to the primary as","options":["Z/10","10 Z","100 Z","Z/100"],"answer":2,"explain":"Step 1: reflected impedance Z' = (N₁/N₂)² Z. Step 2: = 10² Z = 100Z.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
